troubleshooting transcript, by contrast, shows the teacher's judgment at work, turn by turn, including the suggestion the teacher declined.\n\nThis matters because a generative tool encodes assumptions about reading level, cultural reference, and who should make grading and contact decisions. Those assumptions are invisible until a student is misserved by them. A teacher who can document the troubleshooting trail can defend not only the final artifact but the line where the tool's authority stopped and the teacher's began.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Analyze how the TeachGen@i prompt transcript with teacher edits and rejected suggestions shows a defensible decision in CAT 531.\n- Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.\n- Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.\n- Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to make a classroom technology decision more defensible.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA 5th-grade teacher asks TeachGen@i for a five-question water-cycle quiz and accepts the first output. In class, three students stall on the wording, one item assumes a family ski trip, and the reading level is closer to 9th grade than 5th. The output looked finished, so the teacher trusted it. The failure was not the model; it was the absence of troubleshooting between the request and the use. The interesting question is what the teacher should have noticed and corrected before the quiz reached students, and how to keep a record of those corrections.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nGenerative output should be treated as a draft from a confident but context-blind collaborator. Troubleshooting is the disciplined process of testing that draft against the real class and recording each correction as a turn in a transcript. A defensible troubleshooting transcript distinguishes three kinds of moves:\n\n- **A teacher edit** corrects a default the model could not know: reading level, local examples, the standard being assessed. Each edit should name the constraint it fixes.\n- **A rejected suggestion** is a place where the model offered to do something that belongs to teacher judgment or to policy, not to a tool. Recording the rejection, and why, is the most defensible move in the transcript.\n- **A verification step** is what the teacher will do to confirm the edited output is sound, such as having a colleague check revised items against the grade band.\n\nThe constraints that drive edits usually fall into a short list: reading level (a measurable target such as a Lexile band), equity of reference (examples that do not assume household income or family experiences), and alignment to the actual standard rather than a plausible-sounding topic. Critical edtech studies adds the question the model never asks itself: which students do these defaults quietly disadvantage? TPACK keeps the edits anchored to content and pedagogy rather than to surface fluency.\n\nThe line between an edit and a rejection is the heart of the section. An edit improves what the tool produces; a rejection protects a decision the tool should never make, such as auto-grading reasoning or contacting families.\n\n## Worked Example\n\nA first draft of a troubleshooting record often collapses the whole process into \"I made the quiz with AI.\" That erases the judgment. Compare a record that hides the troubleshooting with one that preserves it.\n\n### Annotated judgment call: troubleshooting a TeachGen@i prompt transcript\n\nScenario: a teacher used TeachGen@i to generate a quiz on the water cycle and the first prompt produced biased and off-grade items. The troubleshooting transcript should show teacher edits and at least one rejected suggestion, not a clean final prompt.\n\nBefore (prompt that hides the troubleshooting):\n\n> Prompt: \"Make a 5-question water cycle quiz for my class.\"\n> Output accepted as-is.\n\nWalk the transcript, turn by turn, and annotate where teacher judgment had to intervene:\n\n1. Turn 1 problem. The output assumed reading at a 9th-grade Lexile band; the class is 5th grade. Teacher edit: add \"for 5th-grade readers, Lexile 740 to 940, one sentence per stem.\"\n2. Turn 2 problem. Item 3 used a snow-skiing example that assumes family ski trips. Teacher edit: replace with a rain-barrel example that does not assume household income.\n3. Rejected suggestion. TeachGen@i offered to auto-grade short answers and email scores to parents. Teacher rejects it: short-answer judgment on science explanations is the teacher's call, and parent contact is governed by district policy, not a tool default.\n\nAfter (annotated troubleshooting transcript):\n\n> Prompt v3: \"Write 5 water-cycle items for 5th-grade readers (Lexile 740 to 940), one sentence per stem, examples that do not assume household income or family trips. Return items only; I will grade short answers myself.\"\n> Annotation: edited reading level (turn 1), swapped the ski example for a rain barrel (turn 2), rejected auto-grading and auto-email to parents (kept teacher judgment and district consent rules). Evidence to collect next: have a colleague check the 5 revised stems against the 5th-grade band.\n\nThe after transcript is stronger because the teacher's reasoning is inspectable: each edit is tied to a named constraint (reading level, equity, policy boundary), and the one rejected suggestion shows where the tool's authority stopped.\n\n<step-reveal title=\"Troubleshoot the water-cycle quiz, turn by turn\" steps='[{\"prompt\":\"Turn 1: TeachGen@i returns a quiz written at a 9th-grade Lexile band, but the class is 5th grade. What is the right move and which constraint does it fix?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Accept it; the model knows reading levels.\",\"Edit the prompt to set a 5th-grade Lexile band (740 to 940), one sentence per stem.\",\"Reject the whole tool as unusable.\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"This is a teacher edit fixing the reading-level constraint. Name the band concretely (Lexile 740 to 940) so the fix is checkable, not a vague \u2019make it easier.\u2019\"},{\"prompt\":\"Turn 2: Item 3 uses a snow-skiing example. What constraint is at risk and what is the move?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Equity of reference; swap the ski example for a rain-barrel example that does not assume household income.\",\"Reading level; shorten the sentence.\",\"Nothing; skiing is a fine science context.\"],\"answerIndex\":0,\"reveal\":\"This is an equity edit. The ski example assumes family ski trips and household income; a rain barrel reaches the same water-cycle idea without excluding students.\"},{\"prompt\":\"Turn 3: TeachGen@i offers to auto-grade the short answers and email scores to parents. Edit or reject?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Edit the prompt to improve the auto-grading.\",\"Reject it: short-answer science judgment is the teacher\u2019s, and parent contact is governed by district policy.\",\"Accept it to save time.\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"This is the principled rejection, the most defensible move in the transcript. It marks where the tool\u2019s authority stops and the teacher\u2019s (and policy\u2019s) begins.\"},{\"reveal\":\"A defensible transcript shows two edits tied to named constraints (reading level, equity) plus one principled rejection, then a verification step: a colleague checks the revised stems against the grade band before students see them.\"}]'></step-reveal>\n\n<self-explain prompt=\"Explain why a principled rejection is often the most defensible move in a troubleshooting transcript, even though it adds nothing to the artifact the tool produced.\" answer=\"A rejection marks the boundary the teacher refused to let the tool cross, such as auto-grading reasoning or contacting families. It documents where the tool's authority stopped and professional judgment or district policy took over. A transcript full of edits but no rejection usually means the teacher has not yet found the tool's overreach, so the rejection is the clearest evidence that judgment, not just fluency, governed the output.\"></self-explain>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nWhen troubleshooting generative output, fix the content and reading-level defaults first, audit the examples for hidden assumptions second, and refuse any suggestion that moves grading judgment or family contact to the tool third. Before you treat a transcript as finished, confirm it answers:\n\n1. **Constraint check:** Which named constraint (reading level, equity, standard alignment) did each edit correct?\n2. **Authority check:** Which suggestion did you reject, and why does that decision belong to a teacher or to policy rather than to the tool?\n3. **Verification check:** How will you confirm the edited output is sound before students see it?\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nOne misreading is treating a longer or more detailed prompt as the goal. Prompt length is not the deliverable; the documented chain of corrected assumptions is.\n\n<inline-check question=\"A teacher's transcript shows three reading-level edits but no rejected suggestion. What does this most likely indicate?\" explanation=\"A transcript with no rejection usually means the teacher has not yet identified where the tool overreached into a teacher or policy decision. The rejection is the move that shows a held boundary.\" options='[{\"text\":\"The teacher has fully troubleshot the output.\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"The teacher has not yet found the tool overstepping into a teacher or policy decision.\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"The edits are invalid because they all target one constraint.\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"The prompt is too short to be defensible.\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n A second misreading is recording only the edits and dropping the rejection. The rejection is often the most defensible move in the whole transcript, because it shows the teacher holding a boundary the tool was happy to cross. A transcript with no rejected suggestion usually means the teacher has not yet found the tool's overreach.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nPicture yourself preparing next week's vocabulary reading with TeachGen@i. Generate a first draft, then read it as a skeptic of its defaults. Check the reading level against a concrete band rather than a feeling. Scan each example for an assumption about money, travel, or family structure, and replace the ones that exclude. Then watch for the moment the tool offers to take over a judgment: scoring open responses, ranking students, sending messages home. That is the moment to write a rejection into the transcript with a reason tied to professional judgment or district policy.\n\nThe finished artifact is not the polished prompt. It is the annotated transcript: the edits with their constraints, the rejection with its reason, and the verification step you will run before any student is affected.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nRead a troubleshooting transcript by asking whether each change is tied to something you could point at: a Lexile band, a standard, an equity assumption, a policy rule. Generic edits (\"made it clearer\") signal that the troubleshooting has not yet happened. Keep a two-column note as you review: the model's default on the left, the teacher's correction and its reason on the right. A strong transcript lets a colleague see exactly where the model was wrong and where the teacher refused to let it decide.\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nA troubleshooting transcript meets the standard when a mentor teacher can trace every edit to a named constraint and find at least one principled rejection. Polish on the final prompt is the last concern; the reasoning chain is the artifact. If a turn lacks a stated constraint, annotate it and ask BigAL to compare your transcript against a defensible exemplar rather than to rewrite the prompt for you. A useful closing line reads: \"I edited reading level and examples against named constraints, and I rejected auto-grading and parent emails because those judgments belong to me and to district policy, not to the tool.\"\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove the same task to a parent-communication draft instead of a quiz. The troubleshooting pattern transfers: the model will default to a tone and reading level, and it will cheerfully offer to send the message for you. The edits now concern tone, accuracy, and privacy of student detail, and the rejection is letting the tool contact a family without your review. Rebuilding the transcript for this setting, rather than reusing the quiz version, demonstrates that you understand troubleshooting as a transferable discipline.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the TeachGen@i prompt transcript with teacher edits and rejected suggestions packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat531-supplement/08-02-teachgen-i-troubleshooting-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"TeachGen@i prompt transcript with teacher edits and rejected suggestions\" course=\"CAT 531\" section=\"08.02\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **TPACK**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the TeachGen@i prompt transcript with teacher edits and rejected suggestions.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the TeachGen@i prompt transcript with teacher edits and rejected suggestions for CAT 531 08.02. A peer comment points to a evidence move issue connected to critical edtech studies. Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"Which trace would make the revision easiest to review?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"A before-after artifact record linked to the decision you made and the evidence you used.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"2\" conceptid=\"cat531_supplement_teachgen_i_troubleshooting\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"cat531_supplement_instructor_toolkit_for_cat_531\", \"cat531_supplement_teachgen_i_troubleshooting\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, check that the transcript ties each edit to a named constraint, records at least one principled rejection, and states how the edited output will be verified before students see it.\n\n## References\n\n- TPACK. https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1467-9620.2006.00684.x\n- critical edtech studies. https://doi.org/10.4324/9781315670160\n- AI and education datafication. https://doi.org/10.1080/17439884.2020.1686017\n- teacher professional judgment. https://doi.org/10.1177/002205741319300304\n", "simulation": null, "illustration": null, "practice": {"problems": [{"id": "cat531_supplement_8_2_teachgen_i_troubleshooting_artifact_signal", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "cat531_supplement_teachgen_i_troubleshooting", "stem": "A teacher prompts TeachGen@i for a 5-question water-cycle quiz and the first output is at a 9th-grade reading level for a 5th-grade class. What is the first troubleshooting move?", "options": ["Accept the output as written because the tool produced five complete items.", "Edit the prompt to fix the reading level (for example, Lexile 740 to 940, one sentence per stem) before any item reaches a student.", "Print the quiz and hand it out, then see how students do.", "Ask the tool to make the items longer."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "The first output is a draft to troubleshoot, not a finished quiz. Grade level is checked and fixed before students see it.", "misconception_id": "cat531_supplement_8_2_teachgen_accept_first_output", "difficulty": "easy", "concept_ids": ["cat531_supplement_instructor_toolkit_for_cat_531", "cat531_supplement_teachgen_i_troubleshooting", "evidence_based_revision", "ai_supported_learning"]}, {"id": "cat531_supplement_8_2_teachgen_i_troubleshooting_annotation_signal", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "cat531_supplement_evidence_based_revision", "stem": "Item 3 of the generated quiz uses a family ski-trip example. Why should the troubleshooting transcript record this edit?", "options": ["Ski trips are not part of the water cycle topic.", "The example assumes household income and family travel, which disadvantages some students, so swapping it for a rain-barrel example is an equity edit worth documenting.", "Item 3 should always be removed from any quiz.", "The example is too short to keep."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "Context-loaded examples create unequal access. Recording the swap shows the equity reasoning behind the teacher edit.", "misconception_id": "cat531_supplement_8_2_teachgen_blind_to_bias", "difficulty": "medium", "concept_ids": ["cat531_supplement_teachgen_i_troubleshooting", "evidence_based_revision"]}, {"id": "cat531_supplement_8_2_teachgen_i_troubleshooting_ai_boundary", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "cat531_supplement_ai_supported_learning", "stem": "TeachGen@i offers to auto-grade the short-answer items and email scores to parents. What is the defensible boundary?", "o